  "question": {
    "text": "If the point $(1, a)$ lies between the straight lines $x+y=1$ and $2(x+y)=3$ then a lies in interval"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "\n$\\left(\\frac{3}{2}, \\infty\\right)$\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\n$\\left(1, \\frac{3}{2}\\right)$\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\n$(-\\infty, 0)$\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\n$\\left(0, \\frac{1}{2}\\right)$",
      "is_correct": true
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<img src=\"assets\\st0173_fca24a06b4cd.png\"><br>\n\nSince, $(1, a)$ lies between $x+y=1$ and $2(x+y)=3$\n$\\therefore$ Put $x=1$ in $2(x+y)=3$.\nWe get the range of $y$. Thus,\n$$\n2(1+y)=3 \\Rightarrow y=\\frac{3}{2}-1=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\nThus ' $a$ 'lies in $\\left(0, \\frac{1}{2}\\right)$"
  }
}